<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- SectionHeading — the heading that opens a section inside a panel: a title, then a hairline running to
     the panel's edge.

       Timeframe ─────────────────────────────────────────────
       ┌──────────────────────────────────┬─────────┐
       │ Tue May 24, 2011                 │  Load   │   ← the section the heading owns
       └──────────────────────────────────┴─────────┘

     ⚠️ THE RULE IS WHY THIS IS A CONTROL AND NOT A STYLE. It replaced SettingsSectionLabelStyle, a
     TextBlock style that could only make the words louder — 12px semibold, letter-spaced, TERTIARY — which
     left every heading in the app quieter than the controls underneath it, reading as a field label that
     happened to be capitalised rather than as the title of anything. A rule is a SIBLING element, and no
     TextBlock style can add one, so the heading had to become a control to get it.

     ⚠️ THE RULE IS DOING THE WORK, not the type size. Carrying the eye across the panel's full width is
     what makes a heading own the band beneath it, which means the title itself does NOT have to get big or
     bright to read as a title — see the size note below for why that matters.

     ⚠️ 14px, NOT 15px, and this is a real ceiling. Every panel that hosts these is a window whose own TITLE
     is 15px semibold at SECONDARY (see Controls/Windows/TemporalWindow and SettingsWindow). A section
     heading at 15px PRIMARY out-weighs the window's own name and starts reading as it. Sit under it.

     ⚠️ SENTENCE CASE, and the caller passes the words as they should appear ("Radar Sites", "Timeframe").
     The old style letter-spaced its text, which reads as small caps and invites callers to SHOUT in the
     string; there is no letter-spacing here, so don't reintroduce upper-case text to compensate for it.

     ⚠️ ONE HEADING RANK FOR THE WHOLE APP. Every section in every Settings tab and every temporal window
     uses this. A second rank is exactly the drift Controls/Styles.xaml warns about — if a panel ever needs
     a quieter sub-heading, add a mode to THIS control rather than a rival style.

     Host contract: Text, and nothing else. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Primitives.SectionHeading"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <Grid ColumnSpacing="10">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/> <!-- the title -->
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>    <!-- the rule, taking whatever is left -->
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <TextBlock x:Name="Title" Grid.Column="0"
                   FontSize="14"
                   FontWeight="SemiBold"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>

        <!-- Centred on the text's line, not on the row: the rule should read as a continuation of the
             title's baseline area, not as a divider sitting under it. -->
        <Border Grid.Column="1" Height="1" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Background="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolidBrush}"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
